#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Test Umoja's window management on a live session, on camera.

Run it from a terminal inside an Umoja session on a test machine, then keep
your hands off the keyboard and mouse until it says it's done. It works with
one monitor or several; tests that need a second monitor are skipped on a
single display. Each test drives the desktop the way a person would, with
real key presses, pointer moves, clicks, drags and scrolls, and then checks
what the compositor, the overview, the dock and the apps report.

    tools/test/umoja-wm-test                    # everything
    tools/test/umoja-wm-test --only hot-corner thumbnail-drag
    tools/test/umoja-wm-test --skip not-responding
    tools/test/umoja-wm-test --list

It writes, by default to ~/umoja-wm-test-<time>/:

    REPORT.md         pass / fail / skip per test, every check, with pictures
    results.json      the same, for a machine (or Claude) to read
    videos/<test>-<output>.mp4   one clip per test and monitor (wf-recorder)
    images/*.png      screenshots of every monitor at the moments that matter
    journal.txt       the session's journal for the run (umoja units)
    log.txt           what the script did, step by step
    setup.json        monitors, workspaces and versions it ran on

Input goes through org.gnome.Mutter.RemoteDesktop (umoja-mir's own virtual
keyboard and pointer, the path a remote desktop app uses), with absolute
pointer positions in layout pixels. The overview and dock answer the test
hooks it reads (OverviewState, TestIcons) only while
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/umoja-demo exists; the script creates and removes it. It
holds an idle inhibitor and stops before any input if the screen is blanked
or locked. Its windows are tools/test/testpad.py, with two desktop files of
its own in ~/.local/share/applications that it removes at the end, and it
puts back every setting it changes.
"""

